1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,617
Count it from the first chord to this morning. One hundred and twenty years. The surface still calls the age late. The house calls it a Tuesday with the lamps to trim.

2
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:09,787
Lhamo is old. She sweeps the stair Asha swept. She was born between two maps. She has outlived both offices that drew them.

3
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:12,340
The Compact is a small office now, with one flag and a radio that still knows the old frequencies. The people at the desks are younger than the argument.

4
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:12,340
A rack stands at the top of the stair. Weapons may be left there. The custom is older than the youngest soldier and simpler than the embargo ever was.

5
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:14,043
There is no guard. Anyone can pick a weapon back up. This year, no one does. Trust here is a practice, not a spell. The rack is full because the practice held today.

6
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:16,170
The roots were taught back, season by season. The stair is open. You can see from the dust of the rim into the gold of the first landing, and you do not need a drill to do it.

7
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:16,170
The steps are the same stones. The city under them is deeper than the first crew imagined when they wrote the word asset. Imani's old margin is framed in the school, in her handwriting: uncounted, and not empty.

8
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:16,170
Put the two years side by side. One lamp room, and a stair just opened. A hundred and twenty years later, halls, a river with its own name, a camp grown small, a door that was never locked.

9
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:15,745
They found the door. They did not find a right to own the house. The people who wanted the river had every decade in which to take it, and the river is still here, cup by cup.

10
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:13,617
Lhamo sets the broom down and asks the question the record was kept to leave you. Which room would you live in. The lamps stay up. The chair is no longer empty.
